title: Atomic Environments
keyword: ENVIRON
manual: structure/#c2-environ
cite: econ_1979 econ_2016

Shows the environment of one atom of the system in the "System" line: the list of its neighbors
with their distances and ECoN weights, which of them are coordinated to it, the geometry of each
shell of coordinated neighbors (with shells as rows), and its radial distribution. It brings
together, for one atom at a time, what the ENVIRON, ECON, COORD and RDF keywords calculate for
all of them. The atom and its coordinated neighbors are marked in the view while the window is
open, and everything is recalculated whenever the atom, the options, the bonds or the geometry
change. During a dynamics run the window keeps the last values, and updates when the run stops.

# Center

"Center" is the atom whose environment is shown, in its color in the view: its species, its
index in the unit cell and, if it is not in the main cell, the lattice vector of its image.
"Pick" lets you choose it by clicking it in the view; in a crystal, this also chooses which
image of the atom is marked. A new system starts with its first atom. In the view, the atom
has an orange halo and its coordinated neighbors have blue halos.

# Neighbors

- "Up to": list the neighbors up to a distance from the atom (in Å), or up to a number of
  shells. A shell is the group of neighbors at the same distance, which may be of different
  kinds.
- "Species": list only the neighbors of one species.
- "Coordinated": which neighbors are coordinated to the atom. "Bonded": the atoms bonded to it
  in the bond list of the system (which the bonds objects draw unless they use their own
  criteria; it includes the bonds added or removed by hand). "Covalent radii": the atoms closer
  than the bond factor times the sum of the covalent radii, with the radii and bond factor of
  the system (those used to calculate its bonds), as COORD does. "ECoN weight": the atoms whose
  weight in the ECoN is at least 0.5, as ECON counts its integer coordination number.
- "Rows": what a row of the table is: a neighbor ("Atoms") or a shell ("Shells").

Right-click the header of the table to write it to the output, to a text file or to the
console.

# Atoms and shells

With "Atoms" the table has one row per neighbor, sorted by distance: its shell, name, index in
the list of non-equivalent atoms ("Id") and in the unit cell ("Cell id"), the lattice vector of
its image next to the marked atom (crystals), the distance, whether it is coordinated, and its
ECoN weight. The ECoN weight of an atom at distance d is exp(1 - (d/d_av)^6), where d_av is the
mean weighted distance to the neighbors of all species (Hoppe's formula, iterated as in ECON).
With "Shells" there is one row per shell and atom (the atoms of a shell that are images of the
same non-equivalent atom), with their number ("Count"), how many of them are coordinated, and
the geometry of the shell; the rows with coordinated neighbors have a blue background, the
color of their halos in the view. The neighbors of the row under the mouse are marked in
yellow in the view, with a line from the atom.

# Geometry

"Geometry", in the table of shells, is the closest ideal shape to the coordinated neighbors of
the shell, among those with the same number of vertices (linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral,
square planar, octahedral, trigonal prismatic, cuboctahedral, and so on). All the coordinated
neighbors listed in the shell count, so the rows of a shell with several kinds of atom give the
same shape, and choosing a species in "Species" leaves only that species in the shells and in
their shapes. The rows with no coordinated neighbors give none, and a shell with no ideal shape
for its number of neighbors (one, for instance) gives a dash. The angles between the neighbors
at the atom are sorted and compared with those of the shape; the number in parentheses is the
root mean square difference, in degrees, zero for a perfect match. Only the angles count, not
the distances. The tooltip gives the next closest shape: two close numbers mean the environment
is halfway between them. There are shapes for 2 to 10, 12 and 14 neighbors. The ideal square
pyramid has the atom above the base (100° from the apex to the base atoms). Each shell has its
own shape: the six neighbors of a distorted octahedron that fall in two shells, four and two,
are a square planar and a linear arrangement, not an octahedron.

# Radial distribution

The plot shows the neighbors of the atom as a function of the distance, each broadened into a
gaussian of unit area whose standard deviation is "Width (Å)", so the area under the curve
between two distances is the number of neighbors between them ("Neighbors per Å", left axis).
The staircase is the running coordination number, the number of neighbors closer than each
distance (right axis). Both count the species chosen in "Species" of the Neighbors section,
and the plot goes as far as the list (in shells mode, four widths beyond the last shell).
Hover the plot to mark in the view, in yellow, the neighbors within two widths of the distance
under the mouse; the line below the plot gives their number and the coordination number at
that distance. Unlike the RDF keyword, this is the distribution around one atom, not normalized
by the density or by 4πr². The section is closed when the window opens.
